Interesting take. I had a similar one when I was coding in C++, to not wrap everything with exception handlers and rather let unexpected crash conditions be surfaced. This leads to more correct code very quickly.

Masking errors may look great at first sight, but it builds technical debt in the long run, with performance degrading slowly but surely.

@yaroslavvb: "Google has an internal 'let it break' essay about a hero engineer whose hard work ends up being a net negative (by masking the underlying issues). My manager sent me that essay when I was trying too hard to get the collective TensorFlow unit test suite green."

The Claude Code source code leak has revealed that the autoCompact mechanism, which attempts to compress context, causes excessive token consumption by retrying indefinitely after failures. In one case, there were as many as 3,272 consecutive failures recorded in the source code comments.

The fix is surprisingly simple: adding a limit of MAX_CONSECUTIVE_AUTOCOMPACT_FAILURES = 3 to stop retries after three consecutive failures. After applying this patch, the user reported that their usage returned to normal, addressing complaints about hitting usage limits too quickly, which were likely due to this bug.

@lydiahallie: "We're aware people are hitting usage limits in Claude Code way faster than expected. Actively investigating, will share more when we have an update!"

Fixed vision encoders like DINO have driven impressive progress in learnable representations for generative modeling, but there is no universal variant across modalities, and they do not scale with the generative model. Self-Flow is introduced as a self-supervised framework that builds learnability directly into flow models, working in a unified and scalable way across image, video, and audio.

Particularly exciting are the gains on video-action prediction, where the overall success rate improves substantially, especially on complex tasks like 'Open and Place'. This research opens up many interesting questions to explore. 

@hila_chefer: "New research from @bfl_ml. Meet Self-Flow: our self-supervised framework for image, audio, video & world models. Do generative models really need DINO to learn strong representations? We propose teaching them directly via a joint framework instead."

If you haven't read this article, you need to. Plex sent 120 remote employees to Honduras for a Survivor-themed retreat with a $500k budget. The CEO ignored warnings about eating vegetables, got E. coli from a salad, and spent the week on an IV while the retreat continued outside his window.

The first challenge involved eating whatever was on the platter, and one participant found and ate a dead tarantula, describing it as 'pretty horrible.' Navy SEAL drills took place on the beach in 100-degree heat, and one employee landed on a fire ant hill in shorts, requiring an injectable antihistamine. A porcupine fell through a shower ceiling onto an engineer, who humorously called the front desk about the 'large rodent thing.' An island day trip with small planes left over 100 people stranded overnight due to a lack of runway lights, with one employee receiving a stranger's IV, all while wearing matching tank tops. Nearly a decade later, most of them still work together and talk about this unforgettable experience. $500k couldn't buy that.

@yrechtman: "Absolute nightmare but totally hilarious."

